## Conclusion

DeepSeek V4.1 Flash is DeepSeek's flagship general-purpose model from their V4 family, released September 10, 2026, featuring 552B-parameter Mixture-of-Experts architecture (8B activated per token on input, 16B on output), 1M token context window, native image understanding, and MIT-licensed open weights. It has strong performance on agentic benchmarks (90.6% on Terminal-Bench 2.1, 42.3% on Terminal-Bench 4.0, 74.2% on DeepSWE v1.1, 88.1% on CyberGym, 72.1% on Apex Bench v1.1) and ranks #1 on Terminal-Bench 4.0 (best verified), Terminal-Bench 2.1 (best verified), DeepSWE v1.1 (best verified), CyberGym (best verified), and GPQA Diamond (best verified). The model costs 97% less than Claude Opus 5.5 on input ($0.30 vs $4 per 1M tokens) and 94% less on output ($1.20 vs $20 per 1M tokens), and is 90% cheaper per test task than GPT-6 Astra ($0.00156 vs $0.01196).

However, detailed public information about DeepSeek V4.1 Flash is limited compared to other frontier models from Western companies. The model's strengths in agentic performance, cost efficiency, and open weights make it a strong contender in the current market, particularly for organizations needing sophisticated AI for high-volume, cost-sensitive agentic workflows and coding tasks. The limited independent benchmark data and lack of specified knowledge cutoff may affect decision-making for some organizations.

For organizations heavily invested in the DeepSeek ecosystem and seeking frontier model capabilities for agentic workflows, coding, and vision tasks, DeepSeek V4.1 Flash could be a viable option. For organizations requiring extensive documentation, benchmark validation, and third-party support, established models from OpenAI, Anthropic, or Google may offer better value and transparency. The open weights and self-hosting capabilities may also be attractive for organizations with strict data sovereignty requirements.

**Overall Assessment:** Exceptional agentic performance and cost efficiency; however, limited public documentation and benchmark data compared to other frontier models, and lack of specified knowledge cutoff may affect decision-making.
